  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\nThis afternoon...Skies are partly cloudy with only a few low\nclouds off the coast, some mid and high clouds streaming in from\nthe southwest and cumulus clouds developing over the mountains.\nIsolated showers over the mtns this afternoon can't be ruled out\nbut the chances are very low. Temperatures are generally a few\ndegrees lower than at this time yesterday.\n\nThe low pressure system west of Point Conception continues to move\nsouth today before turning east tomorrow. It will move through\nSoCal on Thursday and Friday. It will bring further cooling, a\ndeepening marine layer with more low clouds, and widespread\nprecipitation. Showers could spread into coastal areas during the\nafternoon on Wednesday. Those showers are expected to spread \nfarther inland for Wednesday night into Thursday with the greatest\nchances for thunderstorms for late Wednesday night into Thursday.\n\nBetween Wednesday and Thursday night, rainfall across Orange and\nsouthwestern San Bernardino Counties could range from around 1 \ninch near the coast to as much as 2 inches in the mountains. Rainfall\nfor San Diego County will range from around 1 inch in North County\nto 0.50 to 0.75 near the border. High desert areas will receive \naround 0.50 inch of rainfall and the low deserts will receive 0.25\ninch or less. Probabilities for 0.10 inch or more of rainfall \nfrom Wednesday morning through Thursday night exceed 90 percent \nfor the coast, valleys, and mountains. Probabilities for 0.50 inch\nor more exceed 80 percent for the mountains as far south as the \nnorthern half of San Diego County. Probabilities for 1 inch or \nmore range from 15 to 50 percent from the coast to the mountains \nfor Orange and southwestern San Bernardino Counties. Probabilities\nfor 2 inches or more exceed 10 percent for the mountains of \nsouthwestern San Bernardino County.\n\nHigh temperatures on Thursday will range from around 5 degrees\nbelow average near the coast to 15 to 20 degrees below average for\nmountain elevations above 6000 feet. High temperatures on Thursday\nwill be in the 60s to around 70 for the coast and valleys and in\nthe 40s and 50s for the mountains.\n\nPrecip tapers off late Thu into Fri as the low pressure system\nmoves eastward into AZ.\n\nWarming and drying is expected for the weekend into early next\nweek, with some weak offshore winds possible Saturday and Sunday.\nOffshore flow on Saturday could result in northeast to east winds\ngusting 30 to 40 mph in and below mountain passes and canyons as \nwell as the Inland Empire corridor between the Cajon Pass and the \nSanta Ana Mountains. The offshore flow weakens on Sunday.\n\nFor Saturday through Tuesday, high temperatures for coastal areas\nwill range from 5 to locally 10 degrees above average with inland\nareas around 5 degrees below average to locally 10 degrees below \naverage in the mountains. High temperatures for the coast and \nvalleys will be mostly in the mid 70s to lower 80s with the lower \ndeserts in the lower to mid 80s with the mountains in the 50s and \n60s.",
